          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            %em homological algebra
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            up to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism+of+chain+complexes"} quasi-isomorphism of chain complexes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-categorical+localization"} (infinity,1)-categorical localization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+chain+complexes"} category of chain complexes
            at the class of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism+of+chain+complexes"} quasi-isomorphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} derived (infinity,1)-category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
          %p
            When considering nonnegatively graded chain complexes, homological algebra may be viewed as a linearized version of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoids"} infinity-groupoids
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+Dold-Kan+correspondence"} stable Dold-Kan correspondence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebra"} homotopical algebra
            as a nonabelian generalization of homological algebra.
          %p Hence homological algebra is
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The study of a particularly simple sort of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#ToolboxInStableHomotopyTheory"} As a toolbox in stable homotopy theory
                below and the discussion at
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+cube"} cosmic cube
            %li
              %p
                The study of properties of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
                over
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
                of various types, by the use of methods adapted from
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topological
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+theory"} homology theory
            %li
              %p
                A simple fragment of, and toolbox for,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
                — and hence, by extension, unstable
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\bullet(X)
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+chains"} singular chains
                in a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homology"} chain homology
                is the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+homology"} singular homology
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_\bullet(X)
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\bullet(X)
                itself serves as a linearized approximation to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %h3#ToolboxInStableHomotopyTheory As a toolbox in stable homotopy theory
          %p
            With homological algebra being a topic in stabilized
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+chain+complexes"} of chain complexes
            – and thus, by the stable
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "module%20spectrum#StableDoldKanCorrespondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+spectra"} module spectra
          %p
            Historically this modern perspective has developed only in stages out of more “concrete” (more
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} 1-categorical
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The notion of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
                between chain complexes –
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+maps"} chain maps
                which induce
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+groups"} homology groups
                – is the stable version of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalences"} weak homotopy equivalences
                of topological spaces. The
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
                of chain complexes
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(\mathcal{A})
                obtained by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localizing
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Ch
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_\bullet(\mathcal{A})
                at these
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
                is the corresponding
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+maps"} chain maps
                are identified up to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homotopy"} chain homotopy
                between good representatives of these objects. (On the other hand, in more general situations this correspondence is less immediate, and the notion of quasi-isomorphism may not be the best choice; see at
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+theorem"} Whitehead theorem
            %li
              %p
                By the discussion at
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
                the morphisms in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(\mathcal{A})
                are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zig-zags"} zig-zags
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+maps"} chain maps
                that involve
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolutions"} resolutions
                by non-isomorphic but
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphic
                chain complexes. By the various
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures+on+chain+complexes"} model structures on chain complexes
                these resolutions can concretely be constructed as
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+resolutions"} injective resolutions
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+resolutions"} projective resolutions
                and/or more general sorts of resolutions (such as
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+resolutions"} flat resolutions
            %li
              %p
                Notably,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
                between categories of chain complexes may extend to functors on these derived categories by evaluating them on suitable resolutions – accordingly called
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functors"} derived functors
                %em homology groups
                of what abstract homotopy theory calls the “derived functor”, these being the invariants that one can compute.) Much of the theory revolves around computing and characterizing derived functors, for instance in the definition of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
                and hence there are powerful tools for these computations, notably
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+sequences"} spectral sequences
            %li
              %p
                However, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(\mathcal{A})
                is still a rather coarse approximation to the full
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+chain+complexes"} of chain complexes
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              %p
                First of all the derived category is automatically a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension"} suspension
                and
                %em de-suspension
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/looping"}> looping
                ) of chain complexes. Further structure added to these goes by names such as
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enhanced+triangulated+category"} enhanced triangulated category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+derivator"} stable derivator
                is a strong enhancement which encodes basically all the requisite structure for internal computations. Finally, the further promotion of these to
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+model+categories"} stable model categories
                or
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretriangulated+dg-categories"}> pretriangulated dg-categories
                  \/linear
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E+categories"} A-∞ categories
                of chain complexes makes them capture the full information present in the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} Algebra
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
                is
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebra
                over
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+rings"} E-∞ rings
                %em homological algebra
                provides approximations to that: by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+Dold-Kan+correspondence"} stable Dold-Kan correspondence
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
                of
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
                are a presentation for
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Mac+Lane+spectrum"} HR
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+spectra"} module spectra
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity+algebras"} A-infinity algebras
                in
                = succeed "-module" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi HR
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} HR
                %a{:href => "http://www.ncatlab.org/nlab/show/model+structure+on+dg-algebras#RelationToAInfinityAlgebras"} are presented by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebras"} dg-algebras
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebras"} associative algebras
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-infinity+algebras"} E-infinity algebras
                %a{:href => "http://www.ncatlab.org/nlab/show/model+structure+on+dg-algebras#RelationToEInfinityAlgebras"} are presented by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+dg-algebras"} commutative dg-algebras
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid"} commutative algebras
                internal to chain complexes. By variation of this theme a multitude of notions in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebra
                finds their representation in homological algebra, for instance
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-%E2%88%9E+algebras"} L-∞ algebras
                in terms of
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-Lie+algebras"} dg-Lie algebras
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebras"} Lie algebras
                internal to chain complexes.
          %h3#nonabelian_variants Non-abelian variants
          %p
            There are variants of the tools of homological algebra that can also be applied to more non-linear phenomena, see for instance at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            the section
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Dold-Kan%20correspondence#StatementGeneral"} non-abelian case
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebra"} homotopical algebra
            in the wide sense of that term and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+homotopy+theory"} simplicial homotopy theory
            to avoid the crushing of homotopical information that can occur when passing to chain complexes.
