                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1208.3816"}> arXiv:1208.3816
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#ReferencesHistory History
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Weibel"}> Charles Weibel
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A history of homological algebra
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.rutgers.edu/~weibel/HA-history.dvi"} dvi
          %p
            From the introduction of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Collins06"} Collins 2006
          %blockquote
            %p
              The word “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"}> homology
              ” was first used in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topological
              context by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9"} Poincaré
              in 1895, who used it to think about
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
              which were the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundaries"} boundaries
              of higher-dimensional manifolds. It was
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emmy+Noether"} Emmy Noether
              in the 1920s who began thinking of homology in terms of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
              over a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %p
              In 1942 came the first move forward towards homological algebra as we know it today, with the arrival of a
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#EilenbergMacLane42"} paper by Samuel Eilenberg and Saunders MacLane
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hom"} Hom
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ext"} Ext
              defined for the very first time, and along with it the notions of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(A,B)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and covariantly in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %p
              Only three years later this language
              %a{:href => "category#EilenbergMacLane45"} was expanded
              to include
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+equivalence"} natural equivalence
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#CartanEilenberg56"} Cartan and Eilenberg’s book
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functors"} derived functors
              in a systematic way which united all the previous
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+theories"} homology theories
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+theory"} group theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebras"} Lie algebras
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective
              or
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+object"} injective
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+resolution"} projective
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+resolutions"} injective resolutions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom
              being
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+exact+functor"} left exact
              and the first occurrence of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ext
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ext^n
              as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+derived+functors"} right derived functors
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Hom
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom
